      "id": "87a6383b-0937-402e-94c6-d0755cefcec7",
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Section 1.5 – Deduction**\n\nSection 1.5 of *Foundations of Computation* explains how logic can be used to draw conclusions from premises. A premise is a proposition that is accepted as true (or known to be true) for the purposes of an argument; a conclusion is a proposition that can be deduced logically from those premises. If the premises are true, logic forces us to accept the conclusion as true, which is the essence of a *valid* argument",
